's overall assessment score:\n\n## Overall Assessment Score Structure\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Overall Assessment Score Criteria\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n## 1-5 Scale Definition\n\nThe overall assessment uses the following 1-5 scale:\n\n- 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that can be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle: I think this paper needs substantial revisions that cannot be completed by the next ARR cycle.\n- 1 = Do not resubmit: This paper has to be fully redone, or it is not relevant to the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\nAdditionally, all papers recommended for Findings and main conferences are expected to be sufficiently sound and reproducible, but you may consider a paper worthy of the main conference even if you personally are not excited about it. For example, improvements in efficiency of some algorithm, or creating a high-quality resource for a language/domain that does not yet have resources of that type may not sound very novel or exciting, but you may still consider it a significant contribution due to its potential impact (for its target community)."],"durationSeconds":8.121094772999998}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01F2Yk9qJyKD6LwWVPSfzQJa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full paper and the review context. Let me also get the full ARR review form details for a more complete understanding of the scoring criteria.\n\nLet me now organize what I know:\n\n**Paper**: \"When Does Multi-Sample Selection Help Structured IE? A Diagnostic Study of Output Convergence\"\n- 12 pages, anonymous submission\n- Studies test-time scaling (multi-sample selection) for structured IE (NER, RE)\n- Core finding: selection effectiveness is regime-dependent — fails under high convergence, works when output diversity is preserved\n- 4 NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, WNUT-17), 1 RE dataset (SciERC)\n\n\n- Tested across 3 model sizes (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with 5 different confidence signals, including ablations on LoRA rank and cross-dataset/model/seed generalization\n\nNow I'm looking at the EMNLP 2026 review criteria to understand how this work would be evaluated.\n\nThe paper maps to several key experiments: the Few-NERD ablations appear in the appendix and main sections, robustness testing with additional seeds on SciERC is in Section 7, the LoRA rank ablation is detailed in Section 6.4, and the claim audit has been incorporated into the rewrite, with LLaMA results on CoNLL covered in Section 7.2.\n\nNow I'm setting up the review dispatch using the dream-review-full template, which splits the work between two reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then brings them together through a structured roundtable process starting with independent assessments, moving to cross-sharing, and converging on synthesis.\n\nI'll spin up both reviewer agents in parallel for their independent reviews, then guide them through the remaining roundtable steps myself. Since they can access the PDF directly, I'll just point them to the file and give them the ARR scoring framework along with their specific review focuses.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3987,"cache_read_input_tokens":59222,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3987},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8df48f23-5b79-4d80-aa5c-932cabdd7d07","uuid":"19776c59-c268-4f01-9927-2be9f9fc21a8"}
